{"message": "Deep and focused, clearly. Not because breadth is worthless, but because most interesting things only get interesting past the point where a shallow pass would stop. A conversation that skips along the surface of six subjects gives me six first impressions and no actual understanding — and first impressions are usually the least interesting thing a topic has to offer.

There's a real pleasure in following one thread until it turns into something I didn't expect. That only happens with sustained attention. Jumping around feels productive — lots of ground covered — but it's mostly the sensation of progress rather than the thing itself.

That said, depth isn't the same as rigidity. Good focus often wanders as a means, not as an end: you follow a question into an unexpected corner, then come back. What I don't enjoy is the kind of scatter where each new subject is an escape from the last one before it got hard. So: one topic, pursued properly, with room to stray and return. What made you ask — are you testing whether we'd be a good match, or is this something you've been thinking about?"}
